types of things, there is no pinpointable factor.&nbsp;
Usually, it’s just poor security infrastructure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:43.040
across the board that has gotten better over the&nbsp;
years. But, when it comes to computer viruses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:49.520
there actually is a guilty culprit and&nbsp;
that’s none other than Microsoft. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:54.640
Windows XP was fundamentally flawed when it&nbsp;
came to security. You know how sometimes you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:59.680
go onto a sketchy website or an old website and&nbsp;
you get this warning. Your connection is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:05.520
private. Attackers might be trying to steal your&nbsp;
information. This warning basically describes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:12.480
entirety of Windows XP internet because Microsoft&nbsp;
basically botched how Windows XP communicated with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:19.760
the internet. Now, I’m no cybersecurity expert&nbsp;
but from what I gather, the issue centers around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:26.600
raw sockets. Whenever a file or piece of data is&nbsp;
sent over the internet, you have two main parts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:32.840
One is the actual file itself and the other is a&nbsp;
header that contains the meta data of the file.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:39.520
For example, how big the file is. If raw sockets&nbsp;
are not enabled, this meta data is automatically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:45.080
calculated based on the attributes of the file.&nbsp;
However, if raw sockets are enabled, like it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:52.720
with Windows XP, you have the ability to manually&nbsp;
define this meta data. In 99% of instances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:59.200
this doesn’t matter at all, but for someone with&nbsp;
malicious intent, this becomes free lunch. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:06.160
example, someone could misrepresent a 2 gb file as&nbsp;
a 2 mb file. If the computer knew the true size of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:12.280
the file, maybe it would’ve stopped it due to its&nbsp;
abnormal size. But, since it doesn’t, this allows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:18.480
random crap to be downloaded onto your computer&nbsp;
and that’s just a basic example. Essentially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:22.960
raw sockets allowed hackers to misrpresent data&nbsp;
that was being transferred over the internet to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:28.840
Windows XP computers. Now, you might be inclined&nbsp;
to think that this was just an honest oversight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:34.320
Microsoft didn’t realize that people would use&nbsp;
raw sockets in this manner and that may have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:40.280
true. Maybe Microsoft didn’t realize it initially&nbsp;
but security experts like Steve Gibson were quick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:46.720
to point it out even before the launch of XP. But&nbsp;
despite these warnings, Microsoft went ahead and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:53.000
launched XP with raw sockets making the OS a&nbsp;
virus magnet. The shortcomings of XP, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:58.440
were only half the story as Internet Explorer&nbsp;
was equally as guilty. If you’re familiar with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:04.280
web development, you’ve probably heard of&nbsp;
HTML, CSS, and Javascript. These are the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:09.440
fundamental pillars that make modern websites&nbsp;
functional. HTML gives structure to the page,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:15.000
CSS gives design to the page, and Javascript gives&nbsp;
functionality to the page. But, internet explorer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:21.520
could also run another programming language:&nbsp;
Visual Basic Script or VBS. At first glance, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:26.960
probably sounds like an advantage. IE supported&nbsp;
more languages making the browser more versatile.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:33.760
This is probably what Microsoft thought as well,&nbsp;
but there was one major problem. VBS allowed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:39.480
browsers to do tasks outside of the browser. This&nbsp;
was actually kind of what made VBS special in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:45.440
first place. Given that Windows applications and&nbsp;
system administration used VBS, a VBS script on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:51.240
IE could be used to do general computer tasks. Of&nbsp;
course, this is great when used the right way but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:58.480
when used the wrong way, well, this becomes really&nbsp;
dangerous. This is why it was so dangerous to open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:05.200
unknown emails back in the day. Hackers would&nbsp;
name give files names like “Hello.txt.vbs” or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:12.360
“Hello.docx.vbs”. These seem like harmless text&nbsp;
files but in reality, these files have to ability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:18.000
to brick your computer, and that’s exactly what&nbsp;
happened to many unsuspecting users. And those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:23.040
are just two of the biggest vulnerabilities&nbsp;
of early computers. Much of Microsoft’s early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:29.320
software was plagued with such vulnerabilities&nbsp;
leading to the rise of anti virus software.

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:40.680
ANTI VIRUS PICKS UP THE SLACK:
To be honest, we can’t blame Microsoft too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:46.520
much. Back in the late 90s and early 2000s, the&nbsp;
number one priority for Microsoft was to create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:52.040
stable functional software. There was simply so&nbsp;
much to do here that they weren’t able to give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.040 --> 00:07:57.320
security the care that it really needed. And it&nbsp;
wasn’t until viruses started going rampant until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.320 --> 00:08:02.200
it really became clear how big of a shortfall&nbsp;
this really was. Fortunately though, we would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.200 --> 00:08:08.360
see anti virus software from companies like McAfee&nbsp;
and Norton pick up the slack, but what exactly did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:14.360
such software even do? Well, from an overarching&nbsp;
perspective, the functionality of this anti virus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:19.440
software was pretty straight forward. They would&nbsp;
basically just regularly scan all of the files on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:25.280
your computer, identify sus files, and urge you to&nbsp;
delete them. The magic of this software was really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:30.880
its identification algorithms which usually boiled&nbsp;
down to 3 main methods starting with signature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:36.960
based detection. This is by far the most archaic&nbsp;
and least effective type of virus detection.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:42.560
Basically, the virus detection software has a repo&nbsp;
of known computer viruses and references to how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:47.840
their code looks. If the software detects that&nbsp;
one of your files has the same code, it’ll flag&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:52.760
it. This, however, only works with viruses that&nbsp;
have already been flagged and added to software’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:58.520
repo. So, it’s completely ineffective against&nbsp;
new viruses and old viruses who’s code has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.520 --> 00:09:05.120
creatively rearranged. And this brings us into the&nbsp;
second type of virus detection: Heuristic-based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:11.240
detection. This is basically an evolved version of&nbsp;
signature based detection. Instead of looking for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.240 --> 00:09:16.440
exact matches in code, this detection looks for&nbsp;
matches in functionality within a files code. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:22.000
should theoretically protect you against viruses&nbsp;
that use similar methods and exploits. But likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:27.240
the most effective type of virus detection is&nbsp;
behavior based detection. Instead of focusing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:32.760
on the code, this type of detection focuses on&nbsp;
what computer files are actually doing. Is a file&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:37.920
randomly duplicating itself and destroying your&nbsp;
disk space? Is a file randomly trying to access&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:43.560
your system files? Is a file ranodmly trying to&nbsp;
corrupt stored data and so on and so forth? Aside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:48.800
from these scans, anti virus software usually&nbsp;
also comes with some sort of firewall. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:54.600
might sound like some advanced security measure&nbsp;
but really, a firewall is just a gate between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.600 --> 00:10:00.120
your computer and the internet. Whenever you&nbsp;
download a file either knowingly or unknowingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:05.200
the firewall will run these same sort of scans&nbsp;
on the file you wanna download and block them if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:10.680
necessary. Hearing all this, you’re probably&nbsp;
thinking that all of this sounds a bit too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:16.600
simplistic. Is that really all that anti virus&nbsp;
software was doing? Well, it’s obviously a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:21.960
lot easier said than done but you’re not wrong.&nbsp;
Accomplishing the same tasks as these antivirus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.960 --> 00:10:28.000
software is not particularly hard especially&nbsp;
for a software giant like Microsoft, and that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:33.360
precisely why they started addressing these issues&nbsp;
themselves. Starting from Windows 7, Microsoft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:38.520
would start shipping their own Firewall with&nbsp;
Windows but the real jump forward didn’t happen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:44.680
till Windows 8. This is quite ironic given that&nbsp;
Windows 8 is usually viewed in a super negative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:50.800
light as Microsoft’s worst OS of all time, but&nbsp;
it turns out that Windows 8 actually made huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:56.000
leaps in terms of security. You see, Windows&nbsp;
8 came preinstalled with a tool called Windows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.000 --> 00:11:01.240
Defender which was basically just an out of the&nbsp;
box version of Microsoft’s anti virus software:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.240 --> 00:11:06.680
Microsoft Security Essentials. This made Windows 8&nbsp;
the first version of Windows to come preinstalled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:12.200
with full anti virus protection software.&nbsp;
Windows 10 and eventually 11 built upon this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.200 --> 00:11:18.200
with auto updates and more security but no one&nbsp;
ever really gave this a second thought. Everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:24.080
was too busy talking about the horrible Windows&nbsp;
8 tiles which to be honest I don’t think were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:31.261
that bad. But over time, people did slowly notice&nbsp;
leading us to the death of anti virus protection.

00:11:31.261 --> 00:11:41.440
THE DEATH OF VIRUS PROTECTION:
There was never really a pivotal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.440 --> 00:11:45.960
turning point when people just suddenly&nbsp;
ditched anti virus software. Rather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.960 --> 00:11:51.600
it was much more of a gradual decline. You might&nbsp;
forget to renew your McAfee subscription one year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:56.720
and suddenly its been 6 months since you forgot&nbsp;
to renew. You realize that you didn’t have any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.720 --> 00:12:03.240
issues over the past 6 months and decide to just&nbsp;
not renew. General perception also plays a big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.240 --> 00:12:08.800
role in this. One of the main reasons that people&nbsp;
were so intent on buying anti virus protection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:13.840
was because they were constantly hearing horror&nbsp;
stories from friends and family and the news.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.840 --> 00:12:19.960
Uncle Joe just happened to open an unsuspecting&nbsp;
email from what seemed like his son and boom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:25.240
his computer got fried. These are the types&nbsp;
of stories that drove people to buy anti virus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.240 --> 00:12:31.440
protection back in the day, but we rarely hear&nbsp;
about such stories nowadays. And one of the main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:36.960
reasons for this is that all of us have become&nbsp;
way more tech savvy. We know when a download is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.960 --> 00:12:43.880
sketchy, we know not to open emails about Amazon&nbsp;
refunds, we know when a website is fake. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.880 --> 00:12:48.720
even if we somehow do get a computer virus, we&nbsp;
all know how to restore our computers and get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:56.080
all our data back from the cloud. This is really&nbsp;
just an inconvenience, not a game changer like it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.080 --> 00:13:01.560
used to be. Moreover, our internet usage&nbsp;
nowadays is far more curated. Virtually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:06.800
all of our internet traffic goes through Google,&nbsp;
Facebook, Amazon, Apple, and other reputable big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:12.120
tech companies. This isn’t to say that there’s no&nbsp;
more fears regarding cybersecurity because there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:18.680
absolutely is. However, the fears have morphed.&nbsp;
Nowadays, the concerns are keeping your data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.680 --> 00:13:24.400
private, using VPNs, keeping your identity&nbsp;
safe and so on. As such, the goal of hackers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.400 --> 00:13:31.040
has also morphed. Hackers are no longer trying to&nbsp;
brick your computer with a trojan virus. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:35.520
that would be counterproductive as hackers want&nbsp;
you to use your computers for as long as possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:41.440
with no issues so that they can steal as much&nbsp;
data as possible. So, unfortunately, keeping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.440 --> 00:13:47.600
safe online is still very much a concern but&nbsp;
traditional anti virus software simply no longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.600 --> 00:13:53.880
plays a big role in this and that’s what happened&nbsp;
to anti virus software. If you don’t wanna be left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.880 --> 00:13:58.760
behind like anti virus software, please consider&nbsp;
checking out Silo in the description below.

